\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The spectral hypothesis for a finite symmetric matrix}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Over the real numbers, the Rayleigh hypothesis of \ref{fdip-011X} is
the conclusion of \ref{fdip-002R}: a real self-adjoint operator on a
finite-dimensional inner-product space has an orthonormal basis of
eigenvectors. The proof of \ref{fdip-002R} maximizes a Rayleigh quotient
on the compact unit sphere and differentiates. \ref{fdip-011X} does not
use that maximization. It takes the resulting diagonalization as a
hypothesis.}

\p{An algebraic route, not proved in these notes, starts from a root of
the characteristic polynomial over an algebraically closed field. That
root gives a complex eigenvector. A Hermitian pairing makes the
eigenvalue real, and a real kernel vector follows. Until that argument
is written from the notes' definitions, a later application of
\ref{fdip-011X} over the real numbers carries \ref{fdip-002R} as an
explicit hypothesis.}
